1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:07,260
Now, the second topic, so what I did until now, was tell you how to mathematically model graphs in Isabelle,

2
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:13,940
as in, how can we come up with a model of graphs in Isabelle that allows us to prove general theorems about graphs,

3
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:17,720
like for example things about connect components and trees and so forth.

4
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:30,370
The second question is how can we get formalized graphs in such a way that allows an

5
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:32,620
executable algorithm to run on that graph.

6
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:45,440
So one specific thing, one specific operation on graphs that we would need for an executable algorithm that we cannot do

7
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:54,220
on this mathematical model of graphs that I just mentioned in the last video, is the operation of picking a neighbor of a vertex in a graph.

8
00:00:54,220 --> 00:01:01,350
So if you have a vertex, for example, if you remember we had A,

9
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:11,980
we had A and then B and then C, and then we somehow want to pick one neighbor of

10
00:01:11,980 --> 00:01:19,860
A, we don't know which one, we just want to pick one of its neighbors, so one element of its neighborhood.

11
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:27,500
We cannot do that with a mathematical model, because simply the set of pairs, like the set of pairs model in the mathematical model

12
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:33,840
is not executable, the set could be infinite, we don't know if the set is finite or not, so we cannot make it an executable program.

13
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:42,860
There is also no order between the vertices in the way we model them, we just say the neighborhood is just a

14
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:48,640
set of vertices.

15
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:57,920
So if you remember, we had the neighborhood of

16
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:05,090
a vertex U in a graph, to be defined that it takes a graph,

17
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:11,080
so it takes a set like that and returns to you a set of vertices.

18
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:20,220
So there is nothing that orders these, there is nothing that tells you how to choose one neighbor over the other.

19
00:02:23,530 --> 00:02:32,770
So one option to make our graphs usable in the

20
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:43,310
context of an executable algorithm is to use lists to implement the graphs, which means that instead of having

21
00:02:43,310 --> 00:02:46,430
neighborhood return a V set,

22
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:56,540
it can also return a list of vertices as the neighborhood of a given vertex.

23
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:03,260
And then the operation that we're talking about, which is pick a neighbor of a vertex,

24
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:07,510
can just be taking the head of the list.

25
00:03:07,510 --> 00:03:16,090
So for instance, in the first case, in the first definition, the neighborhood of U would be the set,

26
00:03:17,010 --> 00:03:22,490
sorry, the neighborhood of, let me just write that,

27
00:03:23,090 --> 00:03:30,020
so the neighborhood of A, with the first definition, we just get the set B and C,

28
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:34,520
so we don't have an order in between the elements, so we cannot really pick one of them.

29
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:45,020
Because there is no single algorithm to pick an element of a set where there

30
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:48,000
is not an order between the elements of the set.

31
00:03:48,770 --> 00:03:57,820
So either we have that, or we can turn it into a list and instead return a list of elements as our neighborhood.

32
00:03:59,090 --> 00:04:04,390
And that would work, but there are two problems with it.

33
00:04:04,390 --> 00:04:10,750
Firstly, this would be a slow implementation, a very slow one,

34
00:04:11,010 --> 00:04:20,770
because if, for example, I want to know if

35
00:04:20,770 --> 00:04:25,250
a certain vertex is an element of another vertex's neighborhood,

36
00:04:25,250 --> 00:04:30,730
how can you do that check? You can only do that check by going through every element in the list,

37
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:34,560
and that can take linear time,

38
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:42,240
so that's a very slow implementation of neighborhoods, versus, for instance, if we implement the neighborhood as a tree,

39
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:49,710
as a search tree, like a balanced search tree, like what we did in Week 6, in that case,

40
00:04:50,390 --> 00:04:54,730
sorry, in Week 7, in that case, the search would take log n.

41
00:04:54,730 --> 00:05:03,950
So if we fix, so a problem with lists is that it's very slow, and another problem with it is that proofs about

42
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:06,190
graphs as lists are very cumbersome.

43
00:05:08,650 --> 00:05:17,010
So the other option, so we had two options, the first one is just not possible, and the second one is also bad, because it is slow,

44
00:05:17,010 --> 00:05:24,480
and you're fixing, like if you just use, if you always use lists as your neighborhood, then you're fixing yourself,

45
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:30,840
then you're fixing a slow implementation and you cannot change it at any point.

46
00:05:31,980 --> 00:05:35,580
While, for example, what if you want to use a tree instead, which is a bit faster.

47
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:43,850
So the solution is, what I said last time, in last week, is to have an abstract data type.

48
00:05:44,730 --> 00:05:49,810
Basically, what we want to say, what we want to have is, what is the interface of a graph,

49
00:05:50,170 --> 00:05:51,870
and what is the specification of that interface.

50
00:05:51,970 --> 00:05:56,150
And we don't care about how it's implemented, we just want to know the interface and its specification,

51
00:05:56,330 --> 00:06:02,710
and then use that whenever we're reasoning about the graph algorithm, and then later on, when you want to implement the graph,

52
00:06:02,890 --> 00:06:05,990
when you want to implement the neighborhoods, for instance, you can use a tree, or you can use a list,

53
00:06:06,050 --> 00:06:07,390
or you can use whatever you want.

54
00:06:07,390 --> 00:06:13,530
And this allows you to prove, to do the reasoning once and for all of the graph algorithm,

55
00:06:13,530 --> 00:06:15,770
and then the implementation is a separate matter.

56
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:24,620
So basically, we want an abstract data type for graphs which has an adjacency map for the neighborhood.

57
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:30,720
So every vertex maps, that gives you a mapping between every vertex and its neighborhood.

58
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:41,690
And we want, in that abstract data type for directed graphs, to fix the neighborhoods as sets,

59
00:06:42,210 --> 00:06:45,930
so using the set ADT,

60
00:06:45,970 --> 00:06:54,030
but we add to it an operation to pick an element, because as a set here, a lot of times you need to pick a neighborhood,

61
00:06:54,310 --> 00:06:58,530
a neighbor of a given vertex in graph algorithms.

62
00:06:58,530 --> 00:07:07,670
So we want a graph abstract data type that has two things, an adjacency map and a type for neighborhoods as sets that

63
00:07:07,670 --> 00:07:09,270
allows you to pick elements.

64
00:07:10,810 --> 00:07:19,750
So let's start with the latter problem, or the latter requirement, which is we want a set abstract data type that allows you to pick.

65
00:07:19,750 --> 00:07:26,330
So what we do here is we take the set ADT,

66
00:07:26,330 --> 00:07:32,860
if you remember, that was defined as a locale in week 8, so we take that,

67
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:41,350
and then we define another locale, so another ADT, so this is a new ADT,

68
00:07:43,570 --> 00:07:47,050
and that new ADT has the same interface as set,

69
00:07:47,050 --> 00:07:53,780
so that's why we say equal set, plus some other parts of the interface.

70
00:07:53,900 --> 00:08:00,940
So we say a set choose ADT, a set choose abstract data type, is the same as a set plus a selection function,

71
00:08:00,960 --> 00:08:07,920
so this is a function that takes a set and returns to you an element in that set,

72
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:15,500
and it assumes that if the set is non-empty,

73
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:19,220
then isin, if you remember, that was an operation,

74
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:30,740
this comes from the set abstract data type from last week, so the isin operation will always

75
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:34,320
be true for whatever you select from S.

76
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:44,340
So you assume an extra operation select, and you say, if you select an element, that element will always be selected out of the set S.

77
00:08:50,130 --> 00:09:00,110
So this solves one of our problems, which is that we wanted a neighborhood set that allows for choosing an element,

78
00:09:00,110 --> 00:09:08,550
and here, of course, whenever you're using that abstract data type, your proofs should work regardless of what type of element select picks.

79
00:09:08,870 --> 00:09:13,470
So select can pick any, can select the element with the shortest name.

80
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:20,300
We're not saying how it's implemented, we're only saying that the condition is, if the set is non-empty,

81
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:21,900
then it chooses an element of the set.

82
00:09:22,740 --> 00:09:30,060
We're not saying how it does so, so it could choose it by selecting the element with the shortest name,

83
00:09:30,180 --> 00:09:40,630
or the element with the longest name, or the element with the minimal or the maximal alphabetical ordering, and so on.

84
00:09:40,690 --> 00:09:45,350
It doesn't matter how we implement it, or what matters is that it conforms to this assumption.

85
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:51,810
So if you choose an element from a non-empty set, what you get is a member of that element,

86
00:09:52,890 --> 00:09:53,510
of that set.

87
00:09:54,890 --> 00:10:05,390
So now that we have solved our smaller problem, which is how to get an

88
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:07,380
adjacency set for,

89
00:10:09,860 --> 00:10:19,710
adjacency, how can we make sure that the neighborhood of a vertex is a

90
00:10:19,710 --> 00:10:22,610
set abstract data type with choice?

91
00:10:23,750 --> 00:10:26,990
So the next thing is we want to have an abstract data type for the whole graph.

92
00:10:27,610 --> 00:10:30,370
So what is the abstract data type for the whole graph? What is the interface?

93
00:10:30,370 --> 00:10:38,810
So we do it in the same way we did before, so we define a new locale, so this is a new ADT for abstract data types.

94
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:48,880
And then we say that locale is the same as the map ADT, so this is an ADT from week 8,

95
00:10:50,700 --> 00:10:55,600
and the set choose ADT, which is the one that I described just in the last slide.

96
00:10:56,620 --> 00:11:02,780
And then the abstract data type of the graph has a long interface with a lot of functions in it,

97
00:11:02,780 --> 00:11:11,120
because it takes the interface of the adjacency map, so we call this map, adjacency map,

98
00:11:11,770 --> 00:11:16,050
and we call this set 2, we call this ADT vset.

99
00:11:16,450 --> 00:11:21,490
So we get the entire interface of adjacency map, and we get the entire interface of vset.

100
00:11:21,490 --> 00:11:25,170
So from adjacency map we got a couple of functions.

101
00:11:26,230 --> 00:11:32,490
We got the function empty, update, lookup, and adjacency map invariant.

102
00:11:33,150 --> 00:11:38,830
The invariant of adjacency map, these are the things that come from the map ADT.

103
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:50,700
And we also got the interface of the vset ADT,

104
00:11:50,700 --> 00:11:54,060
which is the set choose.

105
00:11:54,860 --> 00:12:08,340
So vset here, we're calling it vset because it's just set of vertices.

106
00:12:11,340 --> 00:12:17,580
So this is from vset, and we get from that the empty set,

107
00:12:17,660 --> 00:12:20,580
insert is in the abstraction function,

108
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:27,680
we call it that, and we get vset, the invariant, and we get the selection function.

109
00:12:28,180 --> 00:12:32,940
And I want you to notice here in this table that the functions that come from the original ADT

110
00:12:32,940 --> 00:12:38,020
will be called, for example, adjacency map empty, but then we can rename it to something else,

111
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:43,600
and I advise you to look into the book chapter and associated Isabelle sources

112
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:47,300
to see how this renaming is happening in the actual theory files.

113
00:12:47,880 --> 00:13:03,160
So here you have, this is the original interface, and

114
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:05,660
here we rename it to these things

115
00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:07,780
so it's a bit of a shortened name.

116
00:13:11,060 --> 00:13:16,960
So for the Pair_Graph_Specs, we actually are not adding any operations,

117
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:19,040
we're not assuming any operations.

118
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:25,480
All we're doing is that we say that a graph abstract data type has an interface of a map,

119
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:32,440
an adjacency map, and a set of vertices for each one of the neighborhoods.

120
00:13:33,660 --> 00:13:39,450
And of course, what we want to make sure is that the types of the two abstract data types fit together,

121
00:13:39,590 --> 00:13:43,910
so the two abstract data types, namely the map and the set choose abstract data type,

122
00:13:43,910 --> 00:13:46,950
we want to make sure that they both talk about the same types.

123
00:13:47,190 --> 00:13:51,980
So for instance, an adjacency map has a function that looks up

124
00:13:55,790 --> 00:14:05,000
by taking an adjacency map and a vertex and returning to you the v-set

125
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,880
of this neighborhood,

126
00:14:07,020 --> 00:14:10,260
an option v-set, so it's either a Some or a None, so it either has a neighborhood

127
00:14:10,260 --> 00:14:11,360
or it doesn't have any neighborhood.

128
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:16,820
And we want to make sure that this neighborhood type, v-set,

129
00:14:17,020 --> 00:14:26,520
is the same as that type, which is used for specifying the interface,

130
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:35,630
which is used for the signatures of the interface of the set choose abstract data type.

131
00:14:35,750 --> 00:14:39,070
So that's why we have to make sure that this is the same state,

132
00:14:39,070 --> 00:14:43,190
this is a tick v-set, and this is a tick v-set.

133
00:14:43,330 --> 00:14:45,350
We need to make sure that both of them have the same type.

134
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:52,420
So now that we have a graph abstract data type,

135
00:14:53,570 --> 00:15:02,200
what we can do is to implement within that abstract data type

136
00:15:02,840 --> 00:15:04,140
more operations on graphs.

137
00:15:04,140 --> 00:15:14,050
For example, remember here, we

138
00:15:14,050 --> 00:15:15,910
didn't add any operations for the graph,

139
00:15:16,050 --> 00:15:21,210
so we only had the operations for the set of vertices,

140
00:15:22,390 --> 00:15:26,890
sorry, the operations for the map, that is the adjacency map,

141
00:15:27,010 --> 00:15:30,550
and the operations for the set of vertices, which is a neighborhood.

142
00:15:31,530 --> 00:15:33,670
Now we can add on top of that, for example,

143
00:15:33,750 --> 00:15:36,410
a function for the graph that adds an edge.

144
00:15:36,730 --> 00:15:40,530
So if we say add edge g u v,

145
00:15:40,830 --> 00:15:42,990
so if you have a graph and two vertices u and v,

146
00:15:43,070 --> 00:15:46,590
and you want to add an edge from u to v in that graph,

147
00:15:47,290 --> 00:15:48,290
what you do is the following.

148
00:15:48,670 --> 00:15:53,700
You look up in the graph the neighborhood of v,

149
00:15:53,860 --> 00:15:57,190
so you look up in the adjacency map, which is g,

150
00:15:57,190 --> 00:16:01,950
so g is just an adjacency map, so this is an adjacency map.

151
00:16:10,750 --> 00:16:13,830
So you look up the neighborhood of u in that adjacency map,

152
00:16:14,390 --> 00:16:15,590
and then you see what happens.

153
00:16:15,630 --> 00:16:26,780
If it has a neighborhood, then you update the

154
00:16:26,780 --> 00:16:29,900
neighborhood of u,

155
00:16:30,060 --> 00:16:34,680
so you update the entry of u in the adjacency map g.

156
00:16:36,060 --> 00:16:39,160
Instead of having that original neighborhood v's,

157
00:16:39,160 --> 00:16:41,280
you have a new neighborhood, which is v's,

158
00:16:41,580 --> 00:16:44,160
with the extra vertex added to it v,

159
00:16:44,760 --> 00:16:47,860
because remember here we want to add an edge from u to v,

160
00:16:49,370 --> 00:16:51,570
so we wanted to add an edge u to v,

161
00:16:51,670 --> 00:16:55,830
so we look up in the adjacency map the neighborhood of u,

162
00:16:55,830 --> 00:17:00,450
that gives us some neighborhood, which we call v's here,

163
00:17:00,530 --> 00:17:06,390
and if that neighborhood exists, we just add v to it.

164
00:17:06,490 --> 00:17:07,930
If it doesn't have a neighborhood,

165
00:17:07,930 --> 00:17:16,500
then we basically create a new neighborhood by saying,

166
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:20,640
let's start from the empty neighborhood,

167
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:22,180
so this is the empty set,

168
00:17:23,950 --> 00:17:28,350
this is the symbol corresponding for that, v set empty,

169
00:17:29,350 --> 00:17:32,110
and then we insert in the empty set the vertex v,

170
00:17:32,210 --> 00:17:33,690
so then it has a singleton neighbor.

171
00:17:37,330 --> 00:17:41,680
And this can define many functions

172
00:17:42,700 --> 00:17:46,280
within that directed graph abstract data type,

173
00:17:47,560 --> 00:17:50,160
in the same way I did here for adding edges,

174
00:17:50,340 --> 00:17:51,460
so you can have also delete edge,

175
00:17:51,620 --> 00:17:55,000
you can also have a function for, I don't know,

176
00:17:55,260 --> 00:17:58,480
a new, simpler function for the neighborhood,

177
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:01,040
for instance here of the vertex,

178
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:03,620
and many, many other functions.

179
00:18:03,620 --> 00:18:06,820
So the idea that I'm trying to show you here is that

180
00:18:06,820 --> 00:18:09,480
once we have an abstract data type for graphs,

181
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:13,980
you can then implement executable operations on that graph,

182
00:18:15,700 --> 00:18:20,660
assuming that you have executable operations like add edge here,

183
00:18:21,280 --> 00:18:23,720
and this is done assuming that you have

184
00:18:25,330 --> 00:18:27,890
an abstract data type for maps,

185
00:18:28,050 --> 00:18:29,310
which is the adjacency map of the graph,

186
00:18:29,470 --> 00:18:31,730
and an abstract data type for a set choosing within

187
00:18:33,890 --> 00:18:36,830
an abstract data type that allows you to choose

188
00:18:37,730 --> 00:18:39,430
an abstract data type of a set

189
00:18:39,430 --> 00:18:42,550
that allows you also to choose arbitrary elements from that set.

190
00:18:47,140 --> 00:18:49,590
So now, if you remember,

191
00:18:51,310 --> 00:18:53,630
for abstract data types, we always had

192
00:18:54,090 --> 00:19:01,050
a couple of parts of an abstract data type.

193
00:19:01,170 --> 00:19:03,390
So the first thing is, we had an interface,

194
00:19:03,610 --> 00:19:05,770
and the interface for an abstract data type for a graph is there,

195
00:19:05,770 --> 00:19:06,910
so that's the interface,

196
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:17,040
but here we have the other part,

197
00:19:17,100 --> 00:19:19,840
which is the abstraction function

198
00:19:20,660 --> 00:19:23,460
and the invariant of the abstract data type.

199
00:19:23,980 --> 00:19:25,240
So first of all, if you remember,

200
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:29,000
the abstraction function used to map the abstract data type,

201
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:31,480
the type of interest in the abstract data type,

202
00:19:31,480 --> 00:19:34,200
which in our case is going to be just adjacency maps,

203
00:19:34,800 --> 00:19:36,380
it maps it to the mathematical model,

204
00:19:36,380 --> 00:19:39,940
which in our case will be the mathematical model of directed graphs,

205
00:19:40,440 --> 00:19:42,580
so this is the mathematical model,

206
00:19:43,860 --> 00:19:49,220
mathematical directed graph,

207
00:19:51,430 --> 00:19:55,630
and this is the abstraction function,

208
00:19:56,510 --> 00:19:58,530
so this is the abstraction function,

209
00:19:59,260 --> 00:20:02,260
which maps an adjacency map,

210
00:20:03,040 --> 00:20:04,040
which we just call G,

211
00:20:10,330 --> 00:20:14,960
maps it to a set,

212
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:16,620
so again, this is a set comprehension,

213
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:18,240
so it maps it to a set of edges,

214
00:20:18,320 --> 00:20:19,620
so a set of pairs like that,

215
00:20:19,720 --> 00:20:21,280
so here you say,

216
00:20:21,760 --> 00:20:26,080
I'm going to take all u, v pairs,

217
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:32,280
such that if I take the neighborhood of u in the graph G, and remember,

218
00:20:33,240 --> 00:20:37,120
ng is this new neighborhood function that we defined just there,

219
00:20:37,950 --> 00:20:41,870
so if I take the neighborhood of u in the graph G,

220
00:20:41,870 --> 00:20:47,590
and find that v occurs in that neighborhood,

221
00:20:51,050 --> 00:20:53,470
then I'll take u and v and put them in this,

222
00:20:53,570 --> 00:20:57,070
I'll take that pair u and v and put them in this comprehension,

223
00:20:57,710 --> 00:21:00,710
so then you end up with a set of pairs,

224
00:21:00,830 --> 00:21:06,800
like for example, a, b, c, d, and so on,

225
00:21:07,550 --> 00:21:10,260
and then this is basically,

226
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:14,860
this is exactly the type that we wanted for the mathematical directed graph,

227
00:21:14,860 --> 00:21:16,640
which is a set of pairs,

228
00:21:18,380 --> 00:21:23,460
so this digraph abstraction function

229
00:21:23,780 --> 00:21:29,580
is just a function that takes G of type adjacency map

230
00:21:29,580 --> 00:21:33,060
and returns to you a set of pairs,

231
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:36,760
and we have a shorthand notation for this abstraction function,

232
00:21:36,760 --> 00:21:39,220
which is just square brackets G,

233
00:21:39,220 --> 00:21:46,050
so if we want to say the abstraction of a graph abstract data type

234
00:21:46,050 --> 00:21:49,790
into a mathematical directed graph is right square bracket G,

235
00:21:52,720 --> 00:21:54,400
so that's for the abstraction function.

236
00:21:54,900 --> 00:21:57,440
The next part is the invariant,

237
00:21:57,640 --> 00:22:03,680
which is, what is the invariant of the directed graph abstract data type,

238
00:22:03,940 --> 00:22:06,920
so the invariant is going to be a predicate

239
00:22:06,920 --> 00:22:09,410
that again takes a G,

240
00:22:09,410 --> 00:22:13,630
so it's going to be, we'll just call it a graph invariant, graph_inv, so it takes a G,

241
00:22:14,050 --> 00:22:16,930
and tells you that that G,

242
00:22:17,390 --> 00:22:18,790
which is an adjacency map,

243
00:22:20,070 --> 00:22:30,000
conforms to the invariant that we inherited from the adjacency map interface, so this one,

244
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:39,340
and it also has the problem for a property that every neighborhood in it,

245
00:22:39,740 --> 00:22:41,460
so if you look up for any vertex,

246
00:22:41,460 --> 00:22:46,180
if you look up the neighborhood of that vertex in the adjacency map, which is G,

247
00:22:47,560 --> 00:22:50,560
and find that it indeed has a neighborhood,

248
00:22:50,880 --> 00:22:53,420
then that neighborhood will conform to the invariant

249
00:22:53,420 --> 00:22:58,200
that we inherited from the set abstract data type,

250
00:22:58,240 --> 00:23:00,960
so remember that's the second invariant, B-set invariant.

251
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:03,840
So what we basically have is that

252
00:23:04,610 --> 00:23:09,070
that graph is represented as an adjacency matrix,

253
00:23:09,430 --> 00:23:11,330
so as an adjacency mapping,

254
00:23:11,330 --> 00:23:13,530
so for example we have,

255
00:23:15,300 --> 00:23:19,140
so for example A, B, C, D,

256
00:23:19,560 --> 00:23:23,180
and then this one is mapped to a set of neighborhoods,

257
00:23:23,420 --> 00:23:31,550
so for example B, C, and this one is mapped to C, D, and so on,

258
00:23:32,690 --> 00:23:33,810
and what we say is,

259
00:23:34,290 --> 00:23:38,330
so this is, so the graph is this adjacency map,

260
00:23:38,470 --> 00:23:39,630
so this is an adjacency mapping,

261
00:23:46,440 --> 00:23:49,540
and every neighborhood in that adjacency map,

262
00:23:49,540 --> 00:23:51,960
so this one is a B-set,

263
00:23:53,720 --> 00:23:55,700
and this one is a B-set,

264
00:23:56,500 --> 00:24:01,000
and what we're saying is that

265
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:18,540
this entire thing conforms to the adjacency map invariant, and each

266
00:24:18,540 --> 00:24:22,620
one of those conforms to the B-set invariant,

267
00:24:23,270 --> 00:24:25,450
so that's the whole, so that's the,

268
00:24:25,830 --> 00:24:29,650
that's the invariant of a graph,

269
00:24:30,050 --> 00:24:34,170
so if we represent a graph as an adjacency map,

270
00:24:34,170 --> 00:24:36,990
then the whole graph conforms to the adjacency map invariant,

271
00:24:36,990 --> 00:24:41,370
and every neighborhood conforms to the set choose invariant.

272
00:24:43,960 --> 00:24:45,980
And now, what are the conditions,

273
00:24:46,180 --> 00:24:51,720
if you remember, we have a specification on every abstract data type

274
00:24:53,250 --> 00:25:00,650
that specify what the behavior of the different elements in this interface are.

275
00:25:00,710 --> 00:25:03,330
If you remember, we have, so we have,

276
00:25:04,490 --> 00:25:09,650
so the graph abstract data type is the map abstract data type,

277
00:25:11,230 --> 00:25:16,410
and additionally, it is the set choose abstract data type,

278
00:25:16,410 --> 00:25:19,010
and each one of those comes with its own assumptions,

279
00:25:21,510 --> 00:25:25,310
but we also need to add more assumptions to connect both the map,

280
00:25:25,890 --> 00:25:27,470
the two abstract data types,

281
00:25:28,160 --> 00:25:32,400
the map abstract data type, and the set choose abstract data type in the context of a graph.

282
00:25:32,680 --> 00:25:40,090
So, for instance, we want to say that if the graph conforms to the graph invariant, G,

283
00:25:41,580 --> 00:25:47,680
then the abstraction, so this is the set, this is the abstraction function for a B-set,

284
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:50,320
we call it here T-set,

285
00:25:51,600 --> 00:25:57,440
that abstraction function applied to the neighborhood of a vertex U in the graph

286
00:25:57,440 --> 00:26:00,220
is going to be, so this is the executable neighborhood,

287
00:26:00,400 --> 00:26:04,660
so this is the executable neighborhood, which is this,

288
00:26:07,190 --> 00:26:09,650
and as you can see, I'm saying it's written like that.

289
00:26:10,130 --> 00:26:13,710
So the executable neighborhood, when you abstract it to a set,

290
00:26:13,810 --> 00:26:17,520
is going to be the same as the mathematical neighborhood,

291
00:26:17,560 --> 00:26:20,820
which is what we did in the beginning of this video,

292
00:26:21,400 --> 00:26:24,420
applied to the abstraction of the graph and that vertex.

293
00:26:24,780 --> 00:26:26,260
So if you take the executable graph,

294
00:26:27,280 --> 00:26:31,080
apply the executable neighborhood function to it and to a vertex U,

295
00:26:31,540 --> 00:26:33,200
and then abstract the whole thing,

296
00:26:33,760 --> 00:26:40,450
what you get is the same as the mathematical neighborhood function

297
00:26:40,450 --> 00:26:44,270
applied to the abstraction of the graph and the same vertex.

298
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:50,080
And similarly, we say, for instance, that if we add an edge,

299
00:26:51,970 --> 00:26:55,430
so we apply the function addEdge to the executable graph,

300
00:26:55,430 --> 00:26:58,490
the abstract error type graph, the adjacency map graph,

301
00:26:58,710 --> 00:27:00,510
and that edge goes from U to V,

302
00:27:01,150 --> 00:27:04,770
and then we do an abstraction of the whole thing with the square brackets.

303
00:27:05,730 --> 00:27:11,830
If we do that, that will be the same as if we do a set and insertion,

304
00:27:13,220 --> 00:27:15,940
so this is an operation on mathematical sets,

305
00:27:16,100 --> 00:27:19,940
if we apply it to the edge UV and the abstraction of the graph.

306
00:27:20,680 --> 00:27:23,780
And similarly, we say, for instance,

307
00:27:25,080 --> 00:27:28,920
similarly to the other abstract data types that we did in Week 8,

308
00:27:30,530 --> 00:27:34,250
so adding an edge UV to the graph G

309
00:27:34,250 --> 00:27:39,070
will lead to the preservation of the invariant

310
00:27:39,070 --> 00:27:44,170
if that graph already had the invariant holding before the edge was added.

311
00:27:44,520 --> 00:27:53,930
So now that we've seen how to build an abstract error type for graphs,

312
00:27:54,130 --> 00:28:01,650
which would allow us to use graphs in the context of executable algorithms

313
00:28:01,650 --> 00:28:07,350
instead of just proving theorems, like sets of pairs,

314
00:28:08,150 --> 00:28:12,590
the next question is how can we make that abstract error type executable?

315
00:28:12,590 --> 00:28:15,020
And the answer is really simple,

316
00:28:15,080 --> 00:28:19,500
because we will just use the same methodology for abstract data types,

317
00:28:20,430 --> 00:28:31,400
where, for instance, you have to instantiate every interface function

318
00:28:31,860 --> 00:28:35,400
with a function that is executable

319
00:28:35,400 --> 00:28:39,680
and that satisfies the properties of the specification.

320
00:28:40,040 --> 00:28:43,220
So for example, whether it's the adjacency map

321
00:28:43,220 --> 00:28:47,380
or the neighborhoods, which are basically set abstract data types,

322
00:28:47,720 --> 00:28:49,240
both of those can be implemented with trees.

323
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:51,940
So they can be implemented with red-black trees,

324
00:28:52,080 --> 00:28:53,480
they can be implemented with 2-3 trees,

325
00:28:54,590 --> 00:28:57,570
and 2-3 trees is basically what we did in last week,

326
00:28:57,750 --> 00:28:59,670
where I showed you how to do sets with them,

327
00:28:59,790 --> 00:29:02,390
so this time we can do the same thing

328
00:29:03,670 --> 00:29:08,150
and do an interpretation of our locale, Pair_Graph_Specs,

329
00:29:09,650 --> 00:29:15,470
but with one of those trees as the implementation of maps, as well as sets.

330
00:29:17,080 --> 00:29:23,730
Then, once we do that, we can run an algorithm on these graph abstract data types

331
00:29:24,560 --> 00:29:26,820
without having to reprove everything for that algorithm,

332
00:29:26,900 --> 00:29:29,480
because once you prove it for that abstract error type,

333
00:29:29,580 --> 00:29:33,360
any implementation will have those properties of what you proved.

334
00:29:36,330 --> 00:29:46,060
So this is the high-level methodology for writing down an executable graph algorithm.

335
00:29:48,590 --> 00:29:51,250
So as I said with the last section,

336
00:29:51,250 --> 00:29:54,710
where we talked about the mathematical model of graphs,

337
00:29:55,270 --> 00:29:57,250
I want you to take a look into the book chapter

338
00:29:57,250 --> 00:30:00,150
and also with the associated theory files,

339
00:30:00,820 --> 00:30:05,440
so you can actually look into the implementation of what we said here.

340
